The '''2025 Burawi leadership election''' was held in 2025 to elect the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]]. It was the first leadership election conducted under '''{{W|ranked-choice voting}}''' (RCV), adopted under the [[Leadership Election Reform Amendment]] endorsed by incumbent [[Marik Sahamin]]. The election resulted in an upset victory for '''[[Hancil Thea]]''' of the '''[[National Party of Burawa]]''', who defeated [[Xasan Garaad]] of the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]] and the incumbent Marik Sahamin of the [[Forward Party of Burawa]] after trailing in first-preference votes.

Thea's victory was the first for the National Party in a leadership election and was widely attributed to the party's broad appeal as a second-preference choice under the new RCV system. The result reflected widespread public dissatisfaction with Sahamin's decade in office, which had been marked by the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal|HSR Scandal]], the [[2022 Sabia operation]], the [[Kwalib reparations]], the [[COVID-19 pandemic in Adonia|pandemic]], and the controversial [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]].

== Background ==

=== The Forward–Socialist split ===

The [[Socialist Party of Burawa]], formed in the 1960s from the remnants of the [[Kadarist Party]] that had led the [[socialist revolution]] of the 1930s, had dominated Burawi politics for over half a century. By the 2010s, the party's long incumbency had produced public frustration: wage growth in the large public sector had stagnated, bureaucratic inertia had slowed policy reform, and the electorate had grown contemptuous of one-party dominance. In 2015, [[Marik Sahamin]], then a young politician within the Socialist Party, broke away to found the '''[[Forward Party of Burawa]]''', promising modernisation and reform. Sahamin won the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]] at the age of 38, making him the youngest [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in the country's history.

The 2015 election was a '''triple election''' — the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]], [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]], and Leadership all fell on the same electoral cycle — and the Forward–Socialist split reshaped the parliamentary landscape. The two parties, which had been a single organisation before 2015, now competed for the same metropolitan electorate.

=== Sahamin's decade in office ===

Sahamin served two five-year terms (2015–2025). i

* '''[[2022 Sabia operation]] (14 February 2022)''': Sahamin authorised a one-day military operation to enter [[Sabia]] and capture '''[[Daahir Guuleed]]''', the country's president, a dictator who had maintained cordial relations with previous Burawi leaders. The operation succeeded — Guuleed was seized and the existing Sabian government was left otherwise intact — but the invasion provoked widespread domestic opposition. Many Burawi regarded the action as an unacceptable violation of another country's sovereignty, inconsistent with the principles of the [[socialist republic]].

* '''[[Kwalib]] reparations''': [[Kwalib]], a small state between [[Kushish]] and Burawa populated by ethnically Burawi people with a distinct [[Hoggaanka|Hoga]] cultural heritage, had gained independence on 8 March 2004 after a protracted border dispute. The original border demarcation had favoured Burawa, retaining valuable iron and uranium deposits on the Burawi side. The dispute had escalated to military occupation of border towns before Burawa capitulated and granted independence, though Kwalib received a much smaller territory than originally claimed. Sahamin negotiated a substantial financial payment to Kwalib as reparations for the occupation and the inequitable borders — a decision that was deeply unpopular with the Burawi public.

* '''[[COVID-19 pandemic in Adonia|Pandemic]] (~2020)''': The global pandemic disrupted the Burawi economy and strained public services, further reducing Sahamin's approval ratings.

* '''[[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]]''': Sahamin's government passed legislation banning domestic commercial flights between all but the major city airports, directing passenger traffic to the [[Burawi High Speed Rail|high-speed rail]] network. The policy was consistent with environmental objectives but was perceived as punitive by residents of rural administrations, who were required to travel to metropolitan airports before accessing the rail network. The act — colloquially referred to as the "flight ban" — became a symbol of metro-centric governance that disregarded the needs of the rural interior.

=== Introduction of ranked-choice voting ===

During his second term, Sahamin endorsed the '''[[Leadership Election Reform Amendment]]''', a constitutional amendment that replaced the previous {{W|first-past-the-post}} system for leadership elections with '''{{W|ranked-choice voting}}'''. The reform was intended to encourage broader coalition-building and reduce the likelihood of minority-mandate leaders. In the event, the new system enabled the outcome Sahamin had not anticipated: a candidate who placed second in first-preference votes could prevail by accumulating second-preference support from across the political spectrum.

