In '''Round 2''', the [[Sago Revolutionary Party]], the [[Reformed Christian Socialist Party (Burawa)|Reformed Christian Socialist Party]], and the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] candidate [[Liban Qaasim]] were eliminated. Industrial Party voters — concentrated in [[Mazar]], [[Khar]], and [[Greater Qallahere|Qallahere]] — redistributed broadly, with a plurality transferring to the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]]. Reformed CSP voters split between the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|CSU]] and the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]].

In '''Round 3''', [[Giorgis Nuur]] of the [[Christian Socialist Union (Burawa)|Christian Socialist Union]] was eliminated. CSU voters — socially conservative, scattered across rural communities with strong [[Burawi Catholic Church|Catholic]] congregations — transferred '''overwhelmingly to the National Party''', reflecting the shared rural base and conservative social values of the two parties. This round pushed Thea past Sahamin and into a close second behind Garaad.

In '''Round 4''', [[Marik Sahamin]] of the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] was eliminated. The critical dynamic of the election became apparent: Forward voters, disillusioned with Sahamin but ideologically rooted in the metropolitan left, were unwilling to rank the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] — from which they had split in 2015 — as their second choice. Instead, a decisive majority ranked the '''National Party''' as the acceptable alternative, viewing Thea as a fresh figure outside the metropolitan establishment. Thea accumulated sufficient transfers to achieve a majority of continuing ballots.

'''[[Hancil Thea]]''' was elected [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] with '''55.4%''' of the final-round vote.

=== Geographic breakdown ===

The geographic distribution of the vote reflected the structural divide in Burawi politics between the [[Administrative divisions of Burawa#Metropolitan Administrations|eight metropolitan administrations]] and the rural interior:

* '''Forward Party''': Carried only the [[Haro (Metropolitan Administration)|Haro]] metropolitan area (population 12.3 million, seat: [[Badalga-Jir]]) and portions of the [[Buran River Valley]] metropolitan area — Sahamin's personal base in the upper [[Buran River|Buran]] region.
* '''Socialist Party''': Carried the majority of the remaining metropolitan administrations — [[South Hudur (Metropolitan Administration)|South Hudur]], [[Kankadadka Capital Region]], [[Sago-Dare (Metropolitan Administration)|Sago-Dare]], [[Greater Lanwaley Metropolitan Administration|Greater Lanwaley]], [[Farada]], and [[Greater Qallahere]].
* '''National Party''': Carried the rural administrations and smaller urban administrations outside the metropolitan areas. Crucially, the National Party was the leading second-preference choice within the metropolitan areas themselves, ensuring Thea's victory in the RCV redistribution despite losing the metropolitan first-preference vote to the Socialists.

The vote split between the Socialist and Forward parties within the metropolitan electorate — a consequence of the 2015 party split — prevented either metropolitan party from achieving an outright majority, enabling the National Party to prevail through the aggregation of second-preference support.

== Aftermath ==

Thea's victory produced a '''divided government'''. While the National Party had won the leadership, the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]] and its coalition partners retained approximately 45% of seats in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]], forming a minority government under Prime Minister [[Iacob Naq]]. The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] retained a further bloc of Lower House seats and continued to control the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]], which operated on a separate three-year electoral cycle and had not been contested in the 2025 election. The National Party lacked a parliamentary majority in either chamber, constraining Thea's legislative agenda.

In the immediate aftermath of his election, Thea employed '''executive powers''' to advance his policy priorities without parliamentary approval. Unable to legislate a repeal of the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]], the new Leader directed the [[Burawi Armed Forces|military]] to open military airfields for domestic civilian flights and instructed the state-owned [[national airline]] to recommence domestic routes. The process of reopening airport infrastructure that had been closed since before the pandemic was slow, but the directive signalled Thea's commitment to rural investment.

[[Marik Sahamin]] announced his intention to remain in politics, standing for his former commune seat in [[Badalga]]-14 within the [[Haro (Metropolitan Administration)|Haro]] metropolitan area. [[Nacan Badalgen]], the [[President of Burawa]] nominated by Sahamin, faced the expiry of his term, enabling Thea to nominate a replacement subject to parliamentary confirmation.

== Significance ==

The 2025 election was the first in Burawi history won by the [[National Party of Burawa]], and the first conducted under {{W|ranked-choice voting}}. It demonstrated that the RCV system — adopted under the [[Leadership Election Reform Amendment]] endorsed by Sahamin himself — could produce outcomes fundamentally different from those of the previous first-past-the-post system. The irony was widely noted in Burawi political commentary.

The election also represented the political rehabilitation of the [[Dhulsarren]] region, which had been stigmatised since the [[Burawi High Speed Rail Scandal|HSR Scandal]] of 2019. Thea's emergence from [[Idri]], an urban administration within the Dhulsarren, and his role as the region's principal political advocate during the post-scandal period, transformed the Dhulsarren from a symbol of corruption into the base of the new national leadership.

